WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.910 align:start position:0%
 
rmt general secretary mick lynch  listening to the transport secretary  joining us now hello to you mr lynch  great to see you thanks for joining us  on the program he was talking about you  what are you going to say in response  oh i didn't hear all of it he seemed to  be saying that railway workers have  received 160 000 pound in subsidy what  he really means is that he's given the  private sector operators that money and  last year  they removed 500 million pounds from  this industry in profit so it's it's his  construction of the way that the railway  industry that is  is shaped at the moment is causing these  problems and he's responsible for this  dispute  in the sense that he has a veto on what  the train operating companies can offer  us it's written into their contracts  that he's directly responsible for  industrial relations and he can dictate  what the terms are and the fact is  they've made no offer on pay towards our  members what they've offered us is mass  redundancies  uh recon recontracting our members on  inferior terms and conditions massive  changes to their work-life balance and  cuts in network rail to the to the  safety regime of up to 50  of the inspection so  he's caused this problem but he's got to  facilitate a way out of it and he could  do that today and if he wants to to  facilitate  a negotiated set i mean i'm very happy  to deal with him but what he won't do is  contact me  or any of my officers or any of the  people responsible for this negotiation  and create a way forward  and the reason he won't is beca because  he says that you're militant that you're  extreme left and he went on to say that  you're not normal because there have  been  160 different  disputes and there is some sort of  dispute or  balancing for a dispute every single day  well it's ironic to be called not normal  by the conservative party who are taking  themselves into the realms of the  extreme right at the moment as we've  seen  in this contest they're saying that  paying tax isn't normal and having free  trade unions with the ability to take  industrial action  isn't normal the people behind me are  ordinary men and women in this country  they work hard to deliver the railway  service and what they don't want to do  is see their wages diluted their terms  and conditions diluted and their  contracts of employment ripped up  so i'm not a militant i'm not even a  member of a political party and i  haven't been for 30 years so  the idea that we're running some  ideological campaign is nonsense frankly  what we want to do is get a settlement  so straightforward industrial dispute on  jobs pay and conditions and we can be we  could do that if grant chaps would allow  the companies to negotiate freely and he  hadn't cut two billion pounds from the  funding of the national railway and two  billion pounds from the london  underground in the last few weeks  is it right is it true what he said that  there have been 160 different  disputes or threats of disputes and  there is some threat every single day  [Applause]  well we deal we deal with over 500  companies in the transport sector  ranging from bus  to um  sorry  fire engine ranging from bus to the  ferries  and lots of other uh entities that we  deal with so we will always defend our  members  not all of those disputes run into  industrial action industrial action is  quite rare on the railway and in the  transport sector  so we we will always work with our  employees to try and get a settlement  to any of the disputes that we're  involved in and what we've got here is a  protracted dispute because of the stance  of this government and because of grant  shap's attitude  and is his approach towards industrial  relations so if  these companies were allowed to act as  all the other companies are yeah we  would get a settlement to this dispute  is it right that he says that two vans  when have to go to every job as opposed  to  even one van being needed but you double  up on everything  no that's a lot of nonsense he doesn't  know what he's talking about we are  talking to network rail about the  deployment of staff every day of the  week there's never been an occasion  where our members refuse to get in a van  with other people was rubbish  okay just to clarify he made the point  and i'm glad you've clarified it for us  um four percent is what you've been  offered uh with a possible four percent  next year as well depending on on uh  changes um to working practices um has  that often been made to your members or  did you reject it  um before uh even offering it to them  well we haven't been offered a  straightforward pay deal what we've been  offered is a set of conditions we've got  to accept nearly 2 000 redundancies  we've got to set accept cuts in the  maintenance regime of up to 50  we've got to accept massive changes to  our members working conditions and the  offer that they've made is conditional  on all of those things and it's a four  percent offer that covers last year and  this year and next year's offer  would take it to a three-year deal  inflation today the retail price index  is 11.8 so it doesn't go anywhere near  resolving the cost of living crisis but  what we want to do first is work out  what the conditions are get a guarantee  of no compulsory redundancies and then  we can work on the pay but people are  being impoverished in this country by  below inflation pay deals and this is  yet another below inflation paid deal  what we do with those offers is consult  our members in mass meetings and we have  done that and they have overwhelmingly  given us the answer that they reject  that offer  and those latest conditions have they  been put to your members  yeah we explained all of the offers to  our members and we brought them and they  said no thanks we want to go on strike  again because the reason i'm pushing you  on this mr lynch is because apparently  um that isn't the case and your members  have not been given the opportunity to  uh offer their opinion on the latest  offer from network rail that again is is  incorrect yeah  they've been offered the opportunity to  offer their opinion through their  representatives the same way that mps  represent their constituents we have a  network of representatives and the offer  is obviously unacceptable because if you  look behind me  our people are out on strike in massive  numbers we've got 43 000 people in this  strike when we started we had just over  40 000 people  railway workers are joining this dispute  so they because they want to go on  strike in response to what the  government is offering  the membership in this dispute has grown  by two and a half thousand since the  last strike action so the the support  for this dispute is growing by people  joining the rmt  in response to what they see as these  paltry offers and the offer of  impoverishment going forward okay eddie  dempsey rmt union negotiator i'm sure  you know him um apparently he's said by  industry sources to have um said we can  see a way through it and are prepared to  postpone the strikes  um is that true  that's completely untrue we can see a  way through it we can see the way to a  negotiated settlement but we've never  said to anyone that we're prepared to  postpone the strikes we take the offers  back to our executive committee we talk  to our members we examine the offer  against  what the dispute is about and the offers  so far have not matched what we need to  settle the dispute and i also say kay  that we've not had an offer from the  train operating companies half the  people in dispute work for train  operators and there is no pay offer  whatsoever there is no job security  offer and all that they've proposed is  they shut every ticket office they put  all of our members on new contracts of  employment just like the p o dispute and  fire and rehire and none of that is  acceptable to anyone in our union  uh it looks like uh well certainly as we  speak liz tross is the favorite to be  the next prime minister um she says  she's had enough the union barons i  think she means you  i've got too much power and that power  will be removed with a change in the law  what would you say in response to her  well i'm not a union baron i'm an  elected officer of this union and the  people behind me elect me and they can  get rid of me if they want to  through the election process  we haven't got too much power the  problem in this country is that trade  unions don't have enough power that's  why so many people are suffering poor  wages that's why there's such an  imbalance in the workplace and it's a  shame that the tory party is descending  into an ultra white right clique at the  moment and liz trust is seeking to take  away human rights and the ability of the  democratic right of people to express  themselves through their trade unions  and industrial action which is one of  the pillars of our democracy it's only  repressive regimes that stop people  going on strike and stop them expressing  themselves and stop them rebalancing  the power in the workplace through that  you're a busy monster i'm going to have  to let you go one final question before  i do that you say you haven't got enough  power how much what more power do you  want  well what we want is the ability to  express ourselves as free trade unions  so that we don't have these oppressive  anti-trade union laws we don't have the  threats of millions of pounds of fines  against trade unions and we don't want  to go back to the 70s where one of the  previous  tory governments put trade unionists in  prison for taking industrial action and  if that's the path we're going down  we're going to live in a very repressive  society we want to act freely as  democratic people with people that live  in a liberal society

